<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec-solutions2">
    <title>Solubility and temperature effects</title>


    <p>
        <term><xref ref="def-solubility" text="custom">Solubility</xref></term>  is the a term that describes the amount of one compound that
        can dissolve into water.
        For instance, at <m>20\,^\circ\text{C}</m>, the solubility of <m>NaCl</m> is
        approximately <m>\frac{35\ \text{g}}{100\ \text{g water}}</m>.
        This means that <m>100\ \text{g}</m> of water can dissolve up to <m>38\ \text{g}</m> of sodium
        chloride.
        If we try to dissolve <m>17\ \text{g}</m> of sodium chloride into <m>100\ \text{g}</m> of water, it
        will dissolve, forming a solution.
        We could still dissolve more sodium chloride into that solution because
        it is not yet saturated.
</p>
<p>
        A <term><xref ref="def-saturatedsolution" text="custom">saturated solution</xref></term>  is one where the maximum possible amount of solute has dissolved into water.
        In the example above where we tried to dissolve <m>17\ \text{g}</m> of sodium chloride
        into <m>100\ \text{g}</m> of water, it was easy to determine that it wasn't saturated
        because <m>\frac{17\ \text{g NaCl}}{100\ \text{g water}}</m> is clearly less than the solubility of <m>\frac{35\ \text{g NaCl}}{100\ \text{g water}}</m> since the amount of water is the same.
        However, could we dissolve <m>17.5\ \text{g}</m> of <m>NaCl</m> into <m>43\ \text{g}</m> of water???
        To determine this we want to convert this ratio into one that has <m>100\ \text{g}</m>
        of water in it, allowing us to compare it to the solubility of <m>NaCl</m> in
        water.
        We do this as shown below: </p>
        <p><me>\frac{17.5\ \text{g}\ NaCl}{43\ \text{g}\ \text{water}} = \frac{x}{100\ \text{g}\ \text{water}}</me>
    </p>

    <p>
        <me>x = 40.7\ \text{g}\ NaCl</me>
    </p>

    <p>
        Since <m>40.7\ \text{g}</m> is more than the solubility of <m>35\ \text{g}</m> of <m>NaCl</m> in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> of
        water, this amount of sodium chloride would not completely dissolve and
        we'd end up with a saturated solution with some undissolved solid sodium
        chloride left over.
    </p>

    <p>
        Changing the temperature often has an impact on how much of a compound
        can dissolve in water.
        The effect differs depending on whether the compound that is being
        dissolved is a solid or a gas.</p>
        <p>
        For most solids, the solubility of a solid increases as the temperature
        increases.
        Thus we can dissolve more solute at higher temperatures.
        If a saturated solution is prepared at high temperatures and the
        temperature is then decreased, we can end up in a situation where we
        have more solid dissolved in water than the solubility limit allows for
        and this is called a <term><xref ref="def-supersaturatedsolution" text="custom">supersaturated solution</xref></term>.
        These solutions are unstable and will spontaneously eject solid from the
        solution causing it to crystallize and reform the solid.
    </p>

    <p>
        The solubility of gases, on the other hand, decreases with increasing
        temperature.
        As the temperature rises, less gas can remain dissolved in water.
        This is part of the reason why opened soda remains carbonated in the
        refrigerator but rapidly goes flat if kept warm.
    </p>
    <exercises>
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercisegroup>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    If the solubility of sodium nitrate is <m>88\ \text{g}</m> sodium
                    nitrate/<m>100\ \text{g}</m> water, then would it be possible to dissolve
                    the following?
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>75\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> water
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>75\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> water--yes
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>95\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> water
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>95\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> water--no
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>44\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>50\ \text{g}</m> water
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>44\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>50\ \text{g}</m> water is equivalent to <m>88\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> water--yes
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>57\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>72\ \text{g}</m> water
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>57\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>72\ \text{g}</m> water is equivalent to <m>79\ \text{g}</m> of sodium nitrate in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> of water--yes
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>5\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>9\ \text{g}</m> water
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>5\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>9\ \text{g}</m> water is equivalent to <m>56\ \text{g}</m> sodium nitrate in <m>100\ \text{g}</m> water--yes
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>
        <exercisegroup>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The solubility listed above is the solubility of sodium
                    nitrate at <m>20\,^\circ\text{C}</m>.
                    At <m>80\,^\circ\text{C}</m> the solubility of sodium nitrate is
                    <m>\frac{148\ \text{g sodium nitrate}}{100\ \text{g water}}</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How many grams of sodium nitrate could dissolve in <m>40.0\ \text{g}</m> of water at <m>80\,^\circ\text{C}</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>59.2\ \text{g}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How many grams of sodium nitrate could dissolve in <m>40.0\ \text{g}</m> of water at <m>20\,^\circ\text{C}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>35.2\ \text{g}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If you prepared the saturated solution in part a and
                        then cooled it to <m>20\,^\circ\text{C}</m>, how much solid
                        sodium nitrate would you expect to crystalize?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>59.2 - 35.2 = 24.0\ \text{g}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
</section>